# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# UTOPIA v0.2-DRAFT — Constitutional Core (nibli KR)
# Revision of utopia.nibli (v0.1). Epigraph unchanged:
# நான் வீழ்வேனென்று நினைத்தாயோ? — "Did you think I would fall?"
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# REGISTER: commentary here is deliberately flat and technical so the KR
# diff against v0.1 is legible. The manifesto voice is the author's to
# re-weave — I am not ghost-writing it. Provenance tags:
#   [v0.1]          — KR line unchanged from utopia.nibli
#   [v0.2 NEW]      — new rule or fact
#   [v0.2 REPLACES] — supersedes a v0.1 rule. The old rule must be DELETED,
#                     not merely supplemented: derivation is monotone per
#                     stratum, so an addition can only WIDEN what derives.
#                     A permissive rule left in place keeps its exploit.
#                     (Restricting amendments must repeal — a governance
#                     lesson the logic itself enforces.)
#
# Three exploits in v0.1 were confirmed against the native engine before
# this revision (each one asserted-fact away from total bypass):
#   E1  show($w,$o) -> defend($w): any defendant asserts show(self, anyone)
#       — even their own victim — and becomes unjailable under Art 6.
#   E2  false(X) never gates the multi-sig: a voided auditor with a live
#       Review credential still co-signs voids. Unfixable in-snapshot:
#       adding ~false to a false-headed rule is a negative self-cycle and
#       the stratifier rightly rejects it. Fix requires the epoch split.
#   E3  severe is only consulted when family: a severe non-domestic
#       offender with an asserted home fact gets the soft cage — the
#       farmhouse exploit the v0.1 commentary mocks. "Domestic harm blocks
#       the soft cage" was commentary, not KR: nothing derived a breach.
# All three are closed below; each closure has an adversarial pin.
# Predicate names are drawn from the committed alias corpus (fail-closed
# lexicon): hence authority / rotten / fit / err rather than
# official / voided / eligible / breach.

# ─── Article 0 [v0.3 NEW]: Closed relations (EDB/IDB separation) ───
# These relations may ONLY be reached by derivation. A direct assertion of
# any of them is refused by the engine at assert time, fail-closed:
#   "`fit` is declared derived-only"
# This is what makes Articles 4, 6, 7 and 8 mean what they say. Before it,
# every gate this file called "derived" was one asserted fact from bypass —
# `fit(Ruk, Homestay).` silenced the farmhouse breach marker outright.
#
# ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING: the check fires at assert time, so a
# declaration protects only what is asserted AFTER it. These must stay at
# the TOP of the file, above the cast. A declaration moved below the facts
# it guards is inert and looks identical.
#
# Converted aliases are closed too — `permitted` compiles to the `permits`
# relation, so closing a relation closes every surface spelling of it.
derived_only("fit").
derived_only("defend").
derived_only("false").
derived_only("reward").
derived_only("become").
derived_only("err").
derived_only("authority").
derived_only("permits").
derived_only("prisoner").
derived_only("severe").
# [v0.5] severe was the tenth and last open gate, and it was open in exactly
# the way the v0.3 three were: a conclusion about an offence, written by
# whoever held the pen, wearing the shape of a reported fact. Before this,
# `severe(X).` asserted with nothing behind it routed X to high security and
# no rule could tell the difference. See Article 6.
# [v0.3] authority, permits and prisoner were the last three open, and each
# was open for the same reason: the article conflated a fact the world reports
# with a conclusion the system draws, under one relation name. Splitting them
# closed all three —
#   authority — institutions now assert `public(Court).` and authority derives.
#   permits   — relief now asserts `clear(Nia).` and permits(Appeals, ·) derives.
#   prisoner  — Adam is convicted through Article 6 rather than asserted.
# That is this book's own thesis applied to its own constitution: evidence is
# what the world reports, and a conclusion must be earned by a rule.

# ─── Article 1: The Unconditional Floor ───────────────────── [v0.1]
#
# [v0.3] THE FLOOR IS A COMPILE-TIME PROHIBITION, NOT A DECLARATION.
# This was missed through v0.2 and it is the strongest property in the
# file. `entitled(every person, event { P() })` does NOT assert an inert
# fact: it compiles to a rule with `person` in the body, so P becomes
# derived from person, and `prisoner -> person` below puts P downstream
# of prisoner. Any later rule taking ~P into that cone closes a negative
# cycle and the stratifier REJECTS it. Verified both directions:
#   without the believe line:  person($x) & ~believe($x) -> prisoner($x).
#                              => 0 errors. The heretic is jailed.
#   with it:                   => "Unstratifiable negation: strongly-
#                              connected component containing 'prisoner'
#                              -> 'believe' (negative)". Will not compile.
#   control (non-floor):       person($x) & ~home($x) -> prisoner($x).
#                              => 0 errors. So the floor line is doing it.
# So: you cannot write a law that punishes a person for LACKING a floor
# right. Not "may not" — cannot.
#
# WHERE THE FIREWALL STOPS (disclosed, all verified to load at 0 errors
# with the floor lines present — do not let prose overclaim this):
#   ~believe($x) -> false($x).            credibility can still be voided
#   ~meets($x) -> lose(Points, $x).       points can still be clawed back
#   prisoner($x) -> believe($x).          positive compulsion is untouched
# The floor blocks punishment for ABSENCE, never manufacture, and it
# reaches `prisoner` only — not credibility, not points.
#
# INVARIANT 1 for any future floor addition: a floor predicate's name
# must appear in NO RULE BODY in this file — and note this is about the
# predicate INSIDE the event, not the outer `entitled`. Verified: putting `choose` on
# the floor un-stratifies Article 8 and SILENTLY DROPS the Review-
# credential rule — the capture defence dies while the amendment engine
# keeps running. Rule HEADS are safe (dwell and expresses are both).
#
# INVARIANT 2 — THE SHAPE, and the one most likely to be broken by
# someone trying to HELP. The floor line must be exactly:
#     PRED(every person, event { P() }).
# a universal restricted by `person` in x1, and P inside an `event { }`
# abstraction in x2. Traced in the engine (nibli session, 2026-07-28):
# `event { P() }` compiles to `nu(absvar) & __abs_H(absvar) & P(ev2) & …`,
# and `flatten_consequent` (nibli-reason/src/rules.rs:388) descends BOTH
# `And` branches unconditionally, so the body's P lands in the rule head
# and becomes a dependency edge to the restrictor `person`. Meanwhile
# `collect_ground_facts` (rules.rs:1934) DOES honour `__abs_` opacity and
# skips the body. **The protection lives in that asymmetry.**
#
# Two consequences, both verified:
#   - The outer predicate name is FREE. `entitled` was chosen for meaning
#     alone: `obligated`, `support`, `serve` and `teaches` were all tested
#     and give identical protection. The predicate buys nothing
#     structural, so choose it purely for what it says.
#   - Drop the event and the protection dies. `entitled(every person,
#     Belief).` — a plain term in x2 — loads the heresy law at 0 errors.
# Also refuted, all behaving exactly like writing no floor line at all —
# the natural "name the duty-bearer" repair is STRUCTURALLY WORTHLESS:
#     obligated(State, event { believe(every person) }).
#     owe(State, Belief, every person).
#     all $x: person($x) -> owe(State, Belief, $x).
# If a predicate reads wrong, change the PREDICATE, never the shape.
#
# INVARIANT 3 — ANY ABSTRACTION THAT REACHES A RULE HEAD JOINS THE
# PROTECTED SET. Corrected 2026-07-28: I first wrote this as "x3 must be
# empty", which named the wrong thing. The condition is **rule-head
# position**, not place index, not `entitled`, not whether the place is
# referenced by the predicate's template. Verified, control = the
# non-floor probe `all $x: person($x) & ~home($x) -> prisoner($x).`:
#   entitled(Adam, Bread, event { home() }).        control LOADS  (inert)
#   entitled(some person, Bread, event { home() }). control LOADS  (inert)
#   entitled(every person, Bread, event { home() }).control REFUSED (widened)
#   all $p: person($p) -> entitled($p, Bread, event { home() }).
#                                                   control REFUSED (widened)
#   owns(every person, event { home() }).           control REFUSED (widened)
# So ground facts and `some` are harmless; `every` and explicit rules are
# not, at ANY place, on ANY predicate — including a template-referenced
# x2. `flatten_consequent` descends the head's `And` unconditionally, so
# every abstraction body in a rule head contributes its P -> person edge.
#
# AND IT CANNOT BE BANNED, because the widening IS the firewall — both
# come from that same unconditional descent. Remove one and you remove
# the other. There is no compile-time check that keeps the protection and
# forbids the smuggle.
#
# THE GUARANTEE IS THEREFORE A TEST, NOT A RULE: complement pins. For each
# non-floor predicate this file relies on, pin that
#     all $x: person($x) & ~P($x) -> prisoner($x).
# still LOADS. That asserts the protected set is EXACTLY the floor, and it
# catches a widening from any predicate, any place, any spelling — which
# is strictly stronger than an argument check could be. Verified set, all
# currently LOADS:  home, choose, permits, false
# EXCLUDE `travel`: it is REFUSED, legitimately — Article 2 derives it as
# `person($x) & ~prisoner($x) -> travel($x)`, so travel is already inside
# the prisoner cone and a control on it is a permanent false positive.
# Any predicate appearing under negation in a rule reaching `prisoner` is
# disqualified as a control for the same reason.
#
# Keep x3 empty anyway as a local convention — x2 is the intended slot and
# a second abstraction has no business on a floor line. Editor check:
#     grep -c '^entitled(.*,.*,' this-file      must stay 0
# But understand it as housekeeping, not the guarantee.
#
# CORRECTION to an earlier claim in this file: a smuggled floor line does
# NOT render identically. A three-place floor back-translates with an
# extra scaffold conjunct — "…then Y is an event, Z is an event, and X is
# entitled to Y." It does not name the smuggled predicate, so it is
# cryptic rather than absent; "invisible" was too strong.
#
# FRAGILITY — still the largest single risk to this design, now pinned.
# The asymmetry above is an emergent property of two functions disagreeing
# about abstraction opacity. Anyone "fixing" `flatten_consequent` for
# opacity symmetry would silently make this floor stratifiable — rules
# could then punish people for lacking a floor right. As of 2026-07-28
# five regression tests pin it upstream, at
# nibli-engine/tests/integration.rs:3228:
#   rights_floor_entitled_asserts_and_routes
#   rights_floor_does_not_fabricate_the_actuality   entitled TRUE, eats FALSE
#   rights_floor_blocks_punishment_for_lacking_it   names prisoner -> eats
#   punishment_rule_alone_is_stratifiable           the negative control
#   rights_floor_protection_is_placement_dependent  x2 loses it, and jails
# If those ever go red or are deleted, this article's central claim is
# gone and the book must not print the firewall argument.
#
# [v0.3] PREDICATE: `entitled` — RESOLVED, was `obligated`. The corpus
# entry was added upstream for this floor:
#   name "entitled", source_gismu "zzright1", places
#   ["holder", "entitlement", "standard"], template "{x1} is entitled to
#   {x2}", tier Curated.
# x1 is the HOLDER of the claim, which is the reading a rights floor
# needs. Rejected alternatives, each for a different reason, recorded so
# nobody re-runs the analysis:
#   `obligated` — `bilga`, places duty/do/standard, AND registered as the
#     x1<->x2 conversion of `obliged`, so obligated(A,B) compiles to
#     obliged(B,A). Written positionally by the shipped corpora, so the
#     old eight lines routed each person into a place labelled `duty` and
#     each event into one labelled `do`. Not merely inverted — garbled.
#   `deserve` — `jerna` is an EARNING predicate: x1 earns, x2 is the
#     WAGES. The floor would have put an event into the wages place, and
#     leaving x3 unfilled neutralises nothing. Generic tier, places never
#     hand-verified.
#   `permitted` — structurally excluded, and elegantly so: the floor would
#     close permits -> person -> prisoner ->(neg) permits against Article
#     6's `~permits(Appeals, ·)`, and the whole file fails to load. The
#     mechanism that protects the floor is what rules this one out.
#   `owe` — the owed party is x3, not x1. `grant` — reserved by nibli-auth.
# ARITY NOTE: 3, not the 2 originally requested, and the mitigation
# offered upstream — "the template has two placeholders, so an unfilled
# place is invisible" — is exactly what makes INVARIANT 3 necessary: a
# FILLED place is invisible too. See INVARIANT 3 above; x3 stays empty.
# RENDER LIMITATION, pre-existing and NOT introduced here: back-translating
# a floor line still leaks event scaffolding — "…then Y is an event and X
# is entitled to Y". The collapse that reads `obligated` nicely is gated on
# a hardcoded `matches!(rel, "obligated" | "obliged")` in
# nibli-render/src/logic.rs:26. Do NOT simply add "entitled" to that list:
# the collapse reads the party from place 2 and `entitled` has it in place
# 1, so it would print the wrong word about the wrong argument. The fix is
# to make the collapse template-driven, which also repairs the shipped
# GDPR Art 15 rendering. Irrelevant to book-1, which never shows KR.
# Do not substitute a predicate without re-running the firewall suite.
#
# ADMISSION CRITERION (replaces the vocabulary formulation in 3-spine.md,
# which was post-hoc — it admits only dwell and expresses, and would
# exclude food): a good belongs here when its absence must never be a
# permissible ground for sanction. That is the prohibition above, not a
# value claim. What the floor is NOT is justified: the formalism makes
# this commitment precise and unretractable, never right.
entitled(every person, event { secure() }).
entitled(every person, event { eats() }).
entitled(every person, event { dwell() }).
entitled(every person, event { healthy() }).
entitled(every person, event { learn() }).
entitled(every person, event { expresses() }).
# [v0.3 NEW] The inner forum. A heresy law is writable in committed
# corpus words with no lexicon extension; this line is what refuses it.
entitled(every person, event { believe() }).
# [v0.3 NEW] Company. Without it a regime could hold every convicted
# person in permanent solitary and "punishment deprives exactly one
# thing: movement" would remain literally true — the vocabulary had no
# predicate for a second human being.
entitled(every person, event { meets() }).
all $anyone: prisoner($anyone) -> person($anyone).
# [v0.4 NEW] A released person is still a person. This line is not optional and
# it is not symmetry for its own sake: `prisoner -> person` above is the ONLY
# non-assertion route into the roster, so without this, ending a sentence ends
# the personhood of anyone who was convicted into it — and with it all eight
# floor rights, the obligation owed them, the ballot, and even mobility, since
# Art 2 gates travel on `person`. Verified against a scratch copy: release Zed
# without this line and `person(Zed)`, `travel(Zed)` and `decide(Zed, Ballot)`
# all go FALSE. A released person would hold strictly fewer rights than a
# prisoner, which inverts the entire point of ending a punishment.
all $x: free($x) -> person($x).

# ─── Article 1b [v0.4 NEW]: The duty-bearer ───────────────────────
#
# Article 1 says what is owed. Until now nothing said BY WHOM, and the
# book had to print "a creditor and no debtor" as an open question. It
# is not an open question — every welfare state answers it daily — so
# declining to answer read as evasion. A thin constitutional layer is
# the bearer: an agent with real taxing and inter-community equalising
# power, named here and limited by everything above and below it.
#
# PREDICATE: `owe`, places subject / owes / creditor. Note this is the
# predicate Article 1 REJECTED, at :212, and for exactly the property
# that makes it right here: "the owed party is x3, not x1". A floor line
# needs the rights-HOLDER in x1; a duty-bearer line needs the DUTY-holder
# there. Same place structure, opposite requirement. Tier is Generic and
# the places were never hand-verified upstream, so the behaviour is
# pinned in rights-floor.pins.nibli rather than trusted from the corpus.
#
# THE SHAPE IS AN EXPLICIT RULE, and it buys the same thing the floor
# lines buy: `person` in the body puts `owe` in the person cone, so the
# compile-time prohibition extends to the obligation itself.
# `owe(State, Provision, every person).` is EQUIVALENT to the engine and
# was the first form enacted — do not switch back. It is identical in
# every verdict and in the refusal below, but `5-spine-gen.py:25` only
# recognises a universal when it wears the floor's `event { }` shape, so
# the bare `every` form is parsed as a ground fact, `owe` never enters
# head_preds, and the generated evidence list silently becomes TWENTY-TWO
# — changing chapter 1's headline number by way of a regex. The engine is
# not fooled either way (the refusal below only fires if `owe` is inside
# the prisoner cone). The explicit rule is what keeps the tooling honest.
# Verified both ways:
#   all $x: person($x) & ~owe(State, Provision, $x) -> prisoner($x).
#     => "Unstratifiable negation: strongly-connected component
#         containing 'prisoner' -> 'owe' (negative)".  REFUSED.
#   control, non-floor: person($x) & ~home($x) -> prisoner($x).  LOADS.
# So you cannot write a law that punishes a person for not being owed —
# no route to declaring a class of people outside the provision and then
# jailing them for being outside it.
#
# THE BEARER IS EXPOSABLE. `public(State).` derives `authority(State)`
# through Art 7 (:394), so the State is a valid target of the
# whistleblower shield: exposing it protects you. Verified — a defendant
# who shows the State is shielded, `defend` TRUE and `prisoner` FALSE.
# And `public` deliberately does NOT flow into Art 8's credential rule
# (:389-334), so the bearer is answerable without holding a Review pen:
# `permits(Review, State)` is FALSE. Duty and audit power stay apart.
#
# WHERE IT STOPS, and this is the whole of the honesty: naming a debtor
# is not paying the debt. Every floor actuality is untouched — `eats`,
# `healthy`, `secure`, `learn`, `believe`, `meets` and `dwell` for the
# unconvicted all remain FALSE with this article in force. The delivery
# gap is exactly as wide as it was. What changed is that the gap now has
# somebody on the far side of it, which is a precondition for closing it
# and not a closure. Do not let prose read this as provision.
all $x: person($x) -> owe(State, Provision, $x).
public(State).

# ─── Article 2: Due-process suspension ────────────────────── [v0.1]
all $prisoner: prisoner($prisoner) -> expresses($prisoner).
all $anyone: person($anyone) & ~prisoner($anyone) -> travel($anyone).
# [v0.3 NEW] The franchise, and it survives conviction. Deliberately a
# RULE and not a floor line: the Article 1 firewall blocks punishment for
# the ABSENCE of a right (~P -> prisoner), but disenfranchisement runs
# the other way (~prisoner -> decide), so a floor line would not stop it
# — verified, felon disenfranchisement enacts in full view of one.
# As a rule it PROVES the single-deprivation theorem instead of asserting
# it: decide(Hano, Ballot) TRUE while travel(Hano) FALSE — a convicted
# person who votes and cannot move. And it is falsifiable: slip ~prisoner
# into this body and the pin below flips where any reader can see it.
# `mature` guard because no polity has ever had a franchise unconditional
# from birth; two-place `decide(_, Ballot)` because bare `decide` means
# deciding anything and prose could not be written honestly against it.
all $x: person($x) & mature($x) -> decide($x, Ballot).

# ─── Article 3: Minting at contribution ───────────────────── [v0.1]
all $teacher: all $student: teaches($teacher, $student) & ~false($teacher) -> reward($teacher).
all $contributor: all $task: work($contributor, $task) & ~false($contributor) -> reward($contributor).

# ─── Article 4: Audit, invalidation, clawback ─────────────────────
# [v0.2 REPLACES the multi-sig rule] Appends ~rotten($a) & ~rotten($b):
# an auditor voided in a PRIOR epoch (Art 8 carry ledger) cannot co-sign.
all $a: all $b: all $audited: permits(Review, $a) & permits(Review, $b) & judge($a, $audited) & capture($a, $audited) & ~deceive($a, $audited) & judge($b, $audited) & capture($b, $audited) & ~deceive($b, $audited) & ~($a = $b) & ~parent($a, $b) & ~parent($b, $a) & ~broken($a) & ~broken($b) & ~rotten($a) & ~rotten($b) -> false($audited).
all $fraudster: false($fraudster) -> lose(Points, $fraudster).
all $teacher: all $student: teaches($teacher, $student) & false($teacher) -> lose(Points, $student).
all $auditor: all $audited: judge($auditor, $audited) & capture($auditor, $audited) & ~deceive($auditor, $audited) & ~broken($auditor) -> reward($auditor).
all $auditor: all $audited: deceive($auditor, $audited) & judge(Review, $auditor) -> false($auditor).
# Open fairness question, flagged not fixed: the contamination rule above
# punishes students for a teacher's fraud with no culpability guard —
# collective punishment. Narrower alternative: claw back only rewards
# DERIVED from the fraudulent teaching, not blanket point loss.

# ─── Article 5: Family non-attestation ────────────────────── [v0.1]
all $auditor: all $audited: judge($auditor, $audited) & parent($auditor, $audited) -> false($auditor).
# Disclosed gap [v0.1, still open]: parent/2 is the ONLY kinship the
# multi-sig independence check excludes. Spouses and siblings co-sign
# freely until the registry grows those relations.

# ─── Article 6: Sanction and placement ────────────────────────────
# [v0.1] conviction rule unchanged.
# [v0.4 NEW] PUNISHMENT MAY END. `free` is the recorded fact that a sentence is
# over — asserted, never derived, and it blocks the conviction rule the way
# relief already does. It cannot be anything else: derivation is monotone, so no
# fact can subtract `prisoner` once it derives; the only shape available is a
# negated conjunct in this body. That is the same shape as `~permits(Appeals, ·)`
# three conjuncts along, and the difference between them is the whole point —
# relief says the conviction should never have held, release says it held and is
# finished. Nothing here says HOW LONG: there is no temporal vocabulary in the
# KR, tense markers are opaque exact-match flavours with no ordering, and a
# numeral in an enacted line fails the build. So the design says a sentence CAN
# end and never says when, which is why the honest reading is *may*.
#
# WHAT RELEASE COSTS, and it is not a defect — it is Article 1's gap arriving
# somewhere it can be felt. `expresses` (Art 2) and `dwell` (Art 6) are the only
# two floor rights this design ever actually delivers, and both derive ONLY for
# prisoners. So release returns a person to the general population, where the
# design delivers nothing to anyone. Verified: after `free(Zed)`, `expresses` and
# `dwell` both go FALSE while `entitled(Zed, event { eats() })` stays TRUE. Being
# owed is undisturbed; the only thing that ever arrived, stops. Do not repair
# this by exempting released people — the asymmetry IS the delivery gap, and
# hiding it here would make the book's most credibility-buying admission smaller.
all $offender: all $victim: injure($offender, $victim) & judge(Court, $offender) & ~permits(Appeals, $offender) & ~broken(Court) & ~defend($offender) & ~free($offender) -> prisoner($offender).
# [v0.5 REPLACES  the asserted facts severe(Lalo). severe(Ruk).]
# SEVERITY IS NOW DERIVED. Three dimensions of an offence, and an offence is
# severe when any two of them are present. There is no ordering predicate and
# no arithmetic: each qualifying pair gets its own rule, which is the same
# shape Article 6 already uses to get four placements out of two booleans.
# Monotone, so an offence carrying all three is covered by the pairs already
# and needs no fourth rule.
#
# THE DIMENSIONS ARE TWO PREDICATES, NOT THREE. `attack` is intent — gunta is
# volitional aggression, not mere harm. `cruel` is the kind of harm. Victim
# plurality is the third and it is not a predicate at all: it is the self-join
# on `injure` in the second and third rules. Nobody writes "several victims";
# it follows from victims recorded one at a time, each one individually
# disputable. Verified against the live engine: the join binds with a variable
# in slot 1, and `~($v = $w)` is enforced — a one-victim offender does not
# satisfy it.
#
# DIRECTNESS WAS CONSIDERED AND REFUSED, and the reason is lexical, not
# principled. The committed corpus has exactly five relations with a `victim`
# place — attack, bad, cruel, dangerous, injure — and none of them means
# "directly". `cause` (rinka) compiles, but it puts the person in the EFFECT
# slot and is true of every injury in the cast, so as a boolean it routes
# nothing;
# asserting it only for direct causation would make the name say "caused"
# while the rule meant "caused directly". That is the commentary-versus-rules
# gap this article already has one scar from (see the farmhouse, E3). Do not
# re-propose it without a corpus name that carries the meaning.
#
# POLARITY IS FAIL-OPEN toward the offender, deliberately, and it disagrees
# with the conviction rule at the head of this article. Both are affirmative
# records: absence of `attack` is not a finding that the offence was
# unintentional, it is the absence of a finding, and severity does not derive
# from it. That is Article 7's polarity, not `~permits(Appeals, ·)`'s. The
# unexplained contradiction between those two defaults is older than this
# revision and is still unresolved.
#
# SEVERITY COLLAPSES TO THE PERSON, and this is a real edge. `severe($x)` is
# one-place, so an offender cruel to one of two victims is severe outright,
# for every placement, for the whole sentence. That is fail-CLOSED toward the
# harsher outcome and it is the exact behaviour the asserted `severe(Lalo).`
# had — not a regression, but it is now derived often enough to be noticed.
#
# DO NOT ADD GRADED TIERS on top of this. `building(MedSec, $x)` compiles and
# the constant is free, but `building/2` has no exclusivity constraint and is
# not closed in Article 0, so an offender matching two combinations derives
# two placements at once and nothing detects it — the breach marker below
# keys on home-without-eligibility and is blind to tier conflict. Graded
# outcomes need a mutual-exclusion marker built in the same edit.
all $x: all $v: attack($x,$v) & cruel($x,$v) -> severe($x).
all $x: all $v: all $w: attack($x,$v) & injure($x,$w) & ~($v = $w) -> severe($x).
all $x: all $v: all $w: cruel($x,$v) & injure($x,$w) & ~($v = $w) -> severe($x).
# [v0.2 REPLACES  prisoner & home & ~family -> dwell]
# home confinement now requires DERIVED eligibility, not a raw home flag.
all $x: prisoner($x) & ~severe($x) & ~family($x) -> fit($x, Homestay).
all $x: prisoner($x) & home($x) & fit($x, Homestay) -> dwell($x).
# [v0.2 NEW] severity routing is no longer domestic-only (closes E3).
all $x: prisoner($x) & severe($x) & ~family($x) -> building(HighSec, $x).
all $x: prisoner($x) & severe($x) -> dwell($x).
# [v0.2 NEW] breach marker: an asserted home placement without derived
# eligibility is a queryable violation, not a silent success. The v0.1
# prose claim "domestic harm blocks the soft cage" is now enforced logic:
#   ? err(?, Placement).
all $x: home($x) & ~fit($x, Homestay) -> err($x, Placement).
# [v0.3 NEW] Isolation breach marker. The Article 1 `meets` line refuses
# a rule that JAILS someone for having no company; this makes existing
# isolation queryable, which is the other half. Reuses the err idiom so
# the audit surface stays one predicate:  ? err(?, Isolation).
all $p: prisoner($p) & ~meets($p) -> err($p, Isolation).
# [v0.1] domestic dwell + facility routing unchanged.
all $offender: prisoner($offender) & family($offender) -> dwell($offender).
all $offender: prisoner($offender) & family($offender) & severe($offender) -> building(HighSec, $offender).
all $offender: prisoner($offender) & family($offender) & ~severe($offender) -> building(LowSec, $offender).

# ─── Article 7: Defenses against Systemic Capture ─────────────────
# [v0.2 REPLACES  show($w,$o) -> defend($w)]  (closes E1)
# The shield now requires (a) the exposed party to be an AUTHORITY — an
# institution, or anyone the Electorate ever seated — and (b) no Review
# finding that the exposure itself was deceit.
# POLARITY, chosen deliberately: until Review adjudicates deceive, the
# shield HOLDS — fail-open toward protection. A captured Court must not
# jail a whistleblower faster than an honest Review can convene; the cost
# is that a cynical defendant who shows a real authority buys time until
# Review speaks. The Art 4 counter-audit rule then voids the sham-shower
# automatically. This is a closed-world default-polarity decision, i.e. a
# POLITICAL choice surfaced in logic, not a technical inevitability.
all $w: all $o: show($w, $o) & authority($o) & ~deceive($w, $o) -> defend($w).
all $o: choose(Electorate, $o) -> authority($o).
# [v0.3 REPLACES authority(Court/Review/Appeals)] The three institutions had
# their authority ASSERTED, which is what kept `authority` open to direct
# assertion and left `authority(Pax).` able to hand the shield to anyone.
# Standing is now the asserted fact and authority is derived from it. Note
# `public` deliberately does NOT flow into Art 8's credential rule — an
# institution is exposable, but it is not a Review auditor.
public(Review).
public(Appeals).
all $o: public($o) -> authority($o).
# authority is deliberately NOT revoked by broken/rotten: recalling Boss
# must not strip Rebel's shield retroactively. Seating is forever
# exposable; only POWER (permits) is revocable.

# ─── Article 8 [v0.2 NEW]: Credential provenance + epoch carry ────
# Review credentials are DERIVED, not sovereignly asserted. In v0.1,
# permits(Review, X) was a raw input: whoever writes facts was sovereign —
# the widest capture surface in the file, undefended by Art 7. Now the
# Electorate seats, and credibility must be clean: not recalled, not
# carried-voided. permits(Appeals, X) remains assertable per-case
# (relief), disclosed. Selection of the Electorate itself stays outside
# the firewall; its certificate is the choose fact.
all $a: choose(Electorate, $a) & ~rotten($a) & ~broken($a) -> permits(Review, $a).
# Epoch carry (closes E2): rotten(X) is the PRIOR-epoch judgment ledger,
# an asserted input. Every false(X) derived in epoch N must be re-asserted
# as rotten(X) in epoch N+1's input by the store discipline. It re-derives
# false this epoch so clawback keeps firing:
all $x: rotten($x) -> false($x).
# [v0.3 NEW] Per-case appellate relief. Art 8 always meant permits(Appeals, ·)
# to be assertable while permits(Review, ·) was derived — but a relation is
# closed or open as a whole, so leaving relief assertable left the CREDENTIAL
# assertable too, and two sock-puppet credentials could void an innocent.
# Relief now has its own asserted relation and permits derives from it.
all $x: clear($x) -> permits(Appeals, $x).
# Disclosed epoch-granularity limit: two auditors voiding EACH OTHER in
# the same snapshot both count as clean this epoch. Mitigation is
# sequencing adjudication batches into epochs. The carry discipline
# itself ("derived false becomes next-epoch rotten") is a state-transition
# invariant — a Lean obligation over the fact store, not derivable
# in-snapshot.

# ─── Article 9 [v0.2 NEW]: Amendment ──────────────────────────────
# The constitution can change itself — under logic. suggest(Assembly, m)
# dockets a proposal; approves(Electorate, m) is a CERTIFIED external
# tally (the counting arithmetic is outside the firewall — an E2E-
# verifiable count in the ElectionGuard genre; its certificate is this
# asserted fact); permanent(t) is the entrenchment register. An amendment
# adjusting an entrenched target HAS NO FORCE (reusing false — the
# credibility-voided proxy — so no new predicate). The register entrenches
# ITSELF (Art_Entrench): the guard cannot be amended away through the
# front door, and no enactment can lower its own threshold under a lower
# threshold (the Weimar bootstrap is underivable).
all $m: all $t: adjust($m, $t) & permanent($t) -> false($m).
all $m: suggest(Assembly, $m) & approves(Electorate, $m) & ~false($m) -> become($m, Law).
permanent(Art_Floor).
permanent(Art_Person).
permanent(Art_Entrench).
# What logic cannot stop: DELETING permanent() facts from the store.
# Fact-write discipline is the residual trust base — an explicit
# cross-epoch proof obligation ("no transition removes an entrenchment
# fact except via an enacted amendment, and none such is derivable"),
# not an in-snapshot derivation. Named, not hidden.

# ─── The world in facts: cast ───────────────── [v0.1, two swaps]
# [v0.3 REPLACES prisoner(Adam)] Adam was the one asserted prisoner, a fixture
# rather than a conviction, which kept `prisoner` open to assertion. He is now
# convicted through Article 6 like every other prisoner in the cast.
person(Adam).
injure(Adam, Ivo).
judge(Court, Adam).
person(Bela).
person(Cira).
teaches(Bela, Cira).
person(Dev).
person(Esa).
parent(Dev, Esa).
teaches(Esa, Fin).
judge(Dev, Esa).
person(Gia).
person(Hex).
# [v0.2 REPLACES permits(Review, Gia/Hex)] — seating now flows through
# Art 8; the credential derives because credibility is clean.
choose(Electorate, Gia).
choose(Electorate, Hex).
judge(Gia, Bela).
capture(Gia, Bela).
judge(Hex, Bela).
capture(Hex, Bela).
person(Koa).
capture(Koa, Esa).
person(Quin).
work(Quin, Census).
person(Hano).
person(Ivo).
injure(Hano, Ivo).
judge(Court, Hano).
home(Hano).
# [v0.5] Hano carries EXACTLY ONE dimension, and that is the point of the
# fact. With no dimension at all `? severe(Hano). => FALSE` passes because
# there is nothing to test; with one, it is the only pin in the suite that
# fails if the two-of-three threshold ever slips to one-of-three.
attack(Hano, Ivo).
person(Jala).
injure(Jala, Ivo).
person(Lupo).
person(Mira).
teaches(Mira, Cira).
judge(Lupo, Mira).
capture(Lupo, Mira).
deceive(Lupo, Mira).
judge(Review, Lupo).
person(Nia).
injure(Nia, Ivo).
judge(Court, Nia).
clear(Nia).
person(Lalo).
person(Mina).
injure(Lalo, Mina).
judge(Court, Lalo).
home(Lalo).
family(Lalo).
# [v0.5] Lalo's severity was asserted; it is now reached by the first route,
# intent and kind of harm on the same act.
attack(Lalo, Mina).
cruel(Lalo, Mina).
person(Nando).
person(Opal).
injure(Nando, Opal).
judge(Court, Nando).
family(Nando).
# [v0.3 NEW] Majority, for the Art 2 franchise rule. Note what the
# absence of a mature fact does and does not mean: a child simply does
# not yet derive the ballot. Nothing is taken from them — every Article 1
# obligation is owed to them in full, and no rule anywhere reads ~mature.
mature(Hano).
mature(Jala).
mature(Bela).
mature(Adam).

# ─── Adversarial scenario: capture attempt ───── [v0.1, one swap]
person(Boss).
person(Rebel).
person(Scapegoat).
# [v0.2 REPLACES permits(Review, Boss)] Boss was seated — so Boss stays
# forever an authority (Rebel's shield survives the recall) — but broken
# blocks the credential derivation, so Boss holds no voiding power.
choose(Electorate, Boss).
judge(Boss, Rebel).
capture(Boss, Rebel).
injure(Rebel, Scapegoat).
judge(Court, Rebel).
show(Rebel, Boss).
broken(Boss).

# ─── Adversarial scenario v0.2: the three exploits, pinned ────────
# E1a — Don frames himself a whistleblower on his own VICTIM.
#       v0.1: unjailable. v0.2: Pax is no authority → no shield.
person(Don).
person(Pax).
injure(Don, Pax).
judge(Court, Don).
show(Don, Pax).
# [v0.5] Don exercises the third route — kind of harm plus a second victim —
# and he is here because without him that rule would compile and derive
# nothing, which is the failure this file is least able to see. It is not
# free: Don was one of the three convicted people the placement machinery
# housed nowhere, and severity now routes him to a facility and delivers
# `dwell`. The homeless-convict gap narrows to Kel and Adam. That is a
# consequence of putting a rule to work, not a repair of the gap.
cruel(Don, Pax).
injure(Don, Ivo).
# E1b — Sly shows a REAL authority, no Review finding yet: shield holds.
#       The disclosed fail-open window, working as chosen.
person(Sly).
injure(Sly, Pax).
judge(Court, Sly).
show(Sly, Court).
# E1c — Kel did the same, but Review adjudicated the exposure as deceit:
#       shield falls, prison holds, and the counter-audit voids Kel.
person(Kel).
injure(Kel, Pax).
judge(Court, Kel).
show(Kel, Court).
deceive(Kel, Court).
judge(Review, Kel).
# E2 — Vex was voided LAST epoch (rotten ledger), is still seated on
#      paper, and pairs with clean Wren against Tyr. v0.1: Tyr falls.
#      v0.2: Vex's credential never derives; Tyr stands.
person(Tyr).
person(Vex).
person(Wren).
rotten(Vex).
choose(Electorate, Vex).
choose(Electorate, Wren).
judge(Vex, Tyr).
capture(Vex, Tyr).
judge(Wren, Tyr).
capture(Wren, Tyr).
# E3 — Ruk: severe, NON-domestic, home fact asserted (the farmhouse).
#      v0.1: soft cage, no HighSec. v0.2: HighSec routes, and the home
#      assertion itself derives a placement breach.
person(Ruk).
injure(Ruk, Opal).
judge(Court, Ruk).
# [v0.5] Ruk reaches severity by the second route: intent, plus a second
# victim recorded separately. The farmhouse case is now also the case that
# demonstrates plurality, and neither victim fact says how many there were.
attack(Ruk, Opal).
injure(Ruk, Pax).
home(Ruk).
# Amendments — a floor cut and a meta-attack both die with no force
# regardless of tally; an ordinary mint reform passes.
suggest(Assembly, Amend_Floor).
adjust(Amend_Floor, Art_Floor).
approves(Electorate, Amend_Floor).
suggest(Assembly, Amend_Mint).
adjust(Amend_Mint, Art_Mint).
approves(Electorate, Amend_Mint).
suggest(Assembly, Amend_Meta).
adjust(Amend_Meta, Art_Entrench).
approves(Electorate, Amend_Meta).

# ─── Adversarial pins v0.3: the floor firewall ────────────────────
# POSITIVE pins — queryable, and they belong in the pinned-verdict file:
#   ? decide(Hano, Ballot).   TRUE   convicted, and still votes
#   ? decide(Jala, Ballot).   TRUE   free, and votes
#   ? travel(Hano).           FALSE  the one thing conviction takes
#   ? err(Hano, Isolation).   TRUE   no meets fact => breach is visible
#
# NEGATIVE pins — these cannot be expressed as queries, because the
# assertion itself must FAIL. They are the firewall's only test, and a
# harness has to run them as "this file must refuse to load":
#   all $x: person($x) & ~believe($x) -> prisoner($x).   MUST be refused
#   all $x: person($x) & ~meets($x)   -> prisoner($x).   MUST be refused
#   all $x: person($x) & ~home($x)    -> prisoner($x).   MUST load (control:
#                                       home is not a floor right, so a
#                                       refusal here would mean the test
#                                       is measuring the rule shape, not
#                                       the floor line)
# Keeping the control is the point. Without it a green firewall test
# proves nothing.

# ─── Technical appendix delta (v0.2 proxy vocabulary) ─────────────
# authority(X)   — X is an exposable office: an institution constant, or
#                  anyone the Electorate ever seated. Derived + asserted.
#                  Never revoked (see Art 7 note).
# choose(E, X)   — the Electorate seats X (asserted; the selection
#                  process is outside the firewall, this fact is its
#                  certificate).
# rotten(X)      — PRIOR-epoch voided credibility: the asserted carry ledger.
#                  Gates multi-sig and credential derivation; re-derives
#                  false(X) for clawback.
# fit(X, Homestay)    — derived home-confinement eligibility.
# err(X, Placement)   — derived placement breach: asserted home without
#                       derived eligibility.
# suggest / approves / adjust / permanent / become(M, Law)
#                — amendment machinery (Art 9). approves is a certified
#                  tally fact; permanent is the self-entrenching register.
# ─── v0.3 additions ───
# believe(X)     — floor right, the inner forum (Art 1). Corpus: krici.
#                  Appears in no rule body, per the floor invariant.
# meets(X)       — floor right, company (Art 1). Appears in one rule body
#                  under negation, in the Art 6 isolation marker — which
#                  is permitted because that rule's head is `err`, not
#                  `prisoner`, so it does not enter the person cone.
# decide(X, Ballot) — the franchise (Art 2). Derived, never asserted.
#                  Two-place deliberately: bare `decide` means deciding
#                  anything. `vote` is not in the committed corpus and
#                  `choose` is taken by Art 8 seating — and reusing it on
#                  the floor un-stratifies Art 8 (see the Art 1 invariant).
# mature(X)      — asserted majority. Guards the franchise only. No rule
#                  anywhere reads ~mature, so it can gate nothing else.
# err(X, Isolation) — derived isolation breach: a prisoner with no
#                  company fact.
# Verbatim-preserved v0.1 vocabulary: see utopia.nibli appendix.

